questions.text:
Study the code below:

```php
<?php

$first_name = "Jabulani";
$selected_language = "IsiZulu";

if($selected_language === "English")
  $output_greeting = "Hello";
else if($selected_language === "Afrikaans")
  $output_greeting = "Hallo";
else if($selected_language === "IsiXhosa")
  $output_greeting = "Molo";
else if($selected_language === "IsiZulu")
  $output_greeting = "Sawubona";
else if($selected_language === "Setswana")
  $output_greeting = "Dumela";

echo "$output_greeting, $first_name";
?>
```

a) What is the output displayed when this code executes as it is? (1 mark)
b) The logic encoded in the if else if statements can be replaced with a series of
   if statements. Which of these two constructs is better for the given scenario? Explain
   your answer. (2 marks)
c) Assume $first_name and $selected_language are assigned values Lesedi
   and Tswana (instead of Jabulani and IsiZulu). What is the output displayed? (1
   mark)
d) Rewrite the code to write a function that accepts a name and a language. The
   function makes use of the switch statement instead of the given if else if to
   generate output (as given in the code). (10 marks)
e) Invoke the function in (d) using appropriate arguments. (1 mark)

questions.correctAnswer:
(a) Sawubona, Jabulani.
(b) An if / else if chain is preferable because the language choices are mutually exclusive and processing stops at the first match.
(c) With Lesedi and Tswana, the code does not match Setswana, so $output_greeting is undefined; the greeting is missing and the attempted output is ", Lesedi" with a warning.
(d) Example:
function greet($name,$language) {
  switch ($language) {
    case 'English': $greeting='Hello'; break;
    case 'Afrikaans': $greeting='Hallo'; break;
    case 'IsiXhosa': $greeting='Molo'; break;
    case 'IsiZulu': $greeting='Sawubona'; break;
    case 'Setswana': $greeting='Dumela'; break;
    default: $greeting=''; break;
  }
  echo $greeting . ', ' . $name;
}
(e) greet('Jabulani','IsiZulu');

